1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,920
 The last visualization application that we're going to take a look at is mailing lists and that's kind of ironic.

2
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,920
 We started with the mailing lists and we're going to end with the mailing lists.

3
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:13,560
 The mailing lists, of course, are from my open source Sakai project, which I love and very proud of.

4
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:20,000
 And so what we're going to do is we're going to crawl the archive of a mailing list and then we're going to do two visualizations.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,200
 One is an activity visualization and another is a word cloud.

6
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:29,720
 So it's probably the more important thing is when I do the demonstration of how the software works.

7
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:33,960
 So this is a large data set, so you got to be careful.

8
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:38,560
 This could spider gmain.org, which is a very free and friendly archive.

9
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:43,920
 This data originally came from gmain.org, but I've got a copy of it.

10
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:50,440
 And so gmain.org is not rate limited, but if everyone who is watching this starts

11
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:53,480
 spidering gmain.org at the same time, you will crash it.

12
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:57,600
 It just doesn't have the horsepower to give you this data as fast.

13
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:03,160
 And so I've got something that can give you the data super fast and has no rate limit on a really good server.

14
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:07,640
 And it's cached all around the world using a technology called Cloudflare.

15
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:11,120
 So please, please, please don't point this at gmain.org.

16
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:15,960
 Point this at the URL here, mvoxdrchuck.net, et cetera, et cetera.

17
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:18,760
 And then you can run this as fast as you like.

18
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:25,440
 Now, another thing to worry about is if you have a metered connection.

19
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:30,600
 So don't do this on a cell phone connection, because you'll pay thousands of dollars, perhaps.

20
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:34,440
 Make sure you run a no cost connection before you start running this.

21
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:36,520
 Because this is going to pull a lot of data down.

22
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:43,160
 If you just start this from scratch and you let it run, on a super fast connection,

23
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:47,560
 downloading the whole thing is probably about four hours.

24
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:55,120
 On my home connection, when I had like about a 10 megabit connection, it took several days.

25
00:01:55,120 --> 00:02:00,920
 And so, so just understand that in this one, it's both fun to deal with a ton of data,

26
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:03,320
 and it's scary to deal with a ton of data.

27
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:04,360
 So this one is big.

28
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:10,160
 This one is, you'll see the process in action, cuz it'll run for a while.

29
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:12,920
 Everything, the things will take a long time.

30
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:16,560
 So here's basically the flow of the data in this particular one.

31
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:23,760
 You are going to have the restartable spider that talks to the API, mvoxdrchuck.net,

32
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:27,720
 which has a scalable copy of all this information.

33
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:33,560
 And again, it's going to do kind of a raw database, not a very clean database.

34
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:34,440
 It's sort of a mess.

35
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:39,120
 It's just enough columns to keep track of whether or not we've got this page or not.

36
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:43,240
 And so this has the ones we've retrieved so far.

37
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:47,600
 And so what Gmail does is it sort of scans down to see where to retrieve next,

38
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:51,440
 gets that, and then starts scanning and then adding things here.

39
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:55,520
 So it just adds it, and then it blows up, and then it comes in again and says, okay,

40
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:58,320
 I'll start here, and then it starts retrieving stuff and fills this in,

41
00:02:58,320 --> 00:02:59,960
 fills this in, fills this in.

42
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:03,600
 And sometimes you put like a delay in this so you don't overwhelm networks or

43
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:05,080
 don't overwhelm servers.

44
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:10,280
 But basically this is pretty much a raw retrieval of the email messages.

45
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:12,040
 And this file can get rather large.

46
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:14,480
 This is the one that's greater than a gigabyte.

47
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:17,480
 Now this data is actually really nasty.

48
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:20,320
 It's email data, the date format's changed.

49
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:25,960
 This is data that lasted from 2004 to like 2012 or '13.

50
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:31,560
 And so this data has got a lot of things wrong with it.

51
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:34,480
 It even has things where people's email addresses changed.

52
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:36,040
 And so it has this mapping file.

53
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:41,000
 This comes along with it, this mapping file that says, here's this one person and

54
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,880
 here are the six email addresses that they used throughout the life of the project.

55
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:50,400
 And so there is a relatively complex, and so this is,

56
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:55,880
 this part here is super slow, very slow.

57
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:58,280
 This part here is slow.

58
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:01,080
 But it'll take like, depending on how fast your computer is,

59
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:03,200
 somewhere between two minutes and ten minutes.

60
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:06,360
 This first part will take days, perhaps,

61
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:08,760
 depending on the speed of your network connection.

62
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:12,560
 And so what Gmodel does is it reads through this, it actually recreates,

63
00:04:12,560 --> 00:04:16,600
 it wipes this out, and recreates index.sqlite every time it runs.

64
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:19,320
 So that you can change any number of things, you can respire things,

65
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:20,120
 you can do whatever.

66
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:24,520
 And often, the cleanup, this is one of those cleanup processes, and

67
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:26,160
 you have to tweak the cleanup process.

68
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:28,840
 You're like, look at your data, like, oh, the cleanup missed something, so

69
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:30,120
 I got to run it again.

70
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:33,720
 So this produces index.sqlite every time it runs.

71
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:36,280
 So this is like two to ten minutes.

72
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:38,200
 Gmodel is two to ten minutes.

73
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:41,800
 And it, like, maps names, and when it's all said and done,

74
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:46,800
 this is a very small, highly normalized, it's a nice data model.

75
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:50,080
 This one here had the, content.sqlite has an ugly data model.

76
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:52,560
 Index.sqlite has a pretty data model.

77
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:54,920
 It's got foreign keys, it's got all this stuff.

78
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:58,200
 And all those things we talked about in the database where it's efficient.

79
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:02,120
 And so in your mind, keep track of how fast it is to scan all the data in

80
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:04,120
 a database with a bad model.

81
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:07,280
 And then watch when you run, like, gbasic, which is a scanner, or

82
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:11,240
 gline, which produces line data, or gword, and watch how fast they run.

83
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:14,400
 They run in, like, a couple of seconds, at the most.

84
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:16,320
 And this runs in two to ten minutes.

85
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:20,160
 And the difference is, is that's because the data is efficiently modeled in

86
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:24,360
 index.sqlite, so you can take a look at that using SQLite browser and

87
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:25,920
 take a look at the data model.

88
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:27,960
 And you'll see it looks just like the stuff we talked about in

89
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:32,320
 the database chapter, it's got foreign keys and all those things.

90
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:34,040
 And so that runs, and you got this.

91
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:36,000
 And then we do our visualizations and

92
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,720
 our analysis from this clean version of all the data.

93
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:41,640
 And so gbasic just loops through and prints some stuff out.

94
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:43,920
 It's a great way to test things.

95
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:47,160
 It's a pretty easy to understand program, and you can take a look at it.

96
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:52,560
 gline does some bucketing and makes some histograms to produce a line graph.

97
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:55,100
 And then gword does a different histogram.

98
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:57,160
 It does a histogram of word frequency, and

99
00:05:57,160 --> 00:06:00,720
 then produces that as the word frequency ends up in gword.js.

100
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:06,880
 And then we have two HTML files that use the d3.js visualization

101
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:09,920
 to produce a line and a word chart.

102
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:14,480
 And so in another video I will show you how this code works,

103
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:16,360
 which is probably more useful than this picture.

104
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:24,560
 But this is a whole bunch of good stuff in this particular application.

105
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:27,600
 And if you really understand everything in here,

106
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:31,640
 you can build a pretty sophisticated data retrieval and analysis pipeline.

107
